#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1500.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097486} :activity/id #uuid "90bcccea-5782-59d1-a524-d6852abe26cd" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048780} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097486 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097837 :tutorial/content "Given a fixed **origin** in the plane (often denoted $O$), and another point $X,$ the vector $\\overrightarrow{OX}$ is called the **position vector** of the point $X.$\n\nSo, the position vector tells us how to move from the origin to the point.\n\nFor example, suppose $\\overrightarrow{OA}=\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\overrightarrow{A X} = \\mathbf{b} .$ How can we find the position vector $\\overrightarrow{OX}$ in terms of $\\mathbf a$ and $\\mathbf{b}?$\n\n![](images/e6/e61003fb62534b04cfbbac07a64e61f6c16e2d464eb35bc5b98654b0603b24db.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we can trace a path from $O$ to $X$ by first moving from $O$ to $A,$ and then from $A$ to $X.$ Therefore,\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{OX} = \\overrightarrow{OA}+\\overrightarrow{AX}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\overrightarrow{OA}=\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\overrightarrow{A X} = \\mathbf{b} ,$ we get\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{O X} = \\mathbf{a} + \\mathbf{b} .\n$$\n\nSo, to find a position vector, we can trace a convenient path from the origin to the point and add the vectors along that path." :tutorial/id #uuid "055562c0-b02b-58fe-93a0-5e216241c04f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2910 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "e10b4911-140a-5838-a16e-13feb12fba7b" :step/math-academy-id 661397 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362445}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097487 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096652 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096451 :question/id #uuid "dccb5bac-e528-5b42-9949-36b3870fdde1" :question/math-academy-id "e-4287" :question/problem "Consider the picture below. Given that $\\mathbf{m}=\\lambda\\mathbf{n}+\\mu\\mathbf{k}$, find $\\lambda$ and $\\mu$.\n\n![](images/b8/b8abf22d14f93a38c4d01f368b8171e4ccfe90086d932f5b58453baa46391405.png)" :question/worked-solution "![](images/76/76b9867a7c1ef484922241ebe44272f0104e406c31310bf36227a13850f50d8b.png)\n\nIn $\\bigtriangleup ONM$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $ON=2|\\mathbf{n}|$;\n\n- $\\overrightarrow{ON}$ and $\\mathbf{n}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overrightarrow{ON}=2\\mathbf{n}$.\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $NM=2|\\mathbf{k}|$\n\n- $\\overrightarrow{NM}$ and $\\mathbf{k}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overrightarrow{NM}=2\\mathbf{k}$.\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{m} & =\\overset{\\to }{ON}+\\overset{\\to }{NM} \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{n}+2\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{m}=\\lambda\\mathbf{n}+\\mu\\mathbf{k}=2\\mathbf{n}+2\\mathbf{k}$. \n\nSince $\\mathbf{n} \\nparallel \\mathbf{k},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{n}$ and $\\mathbf{k}$, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda=2$ and $\\mu=2$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "45b6a076-8795-5510-aabc-62a925826dd0" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186760983} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097165} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097241} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256375} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256377} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256378} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256379} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292807} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302254} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302255} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302256} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302257} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302315} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302316} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302317} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329675} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329676} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329679} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345312} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365574} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403878} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420467} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514754} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514764} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514766} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514769} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514779} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514782} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514788} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514789} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572708} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572725} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186582353} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186735007} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186735008} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186735013} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186735240} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186735249} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186735252} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186735258} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186735262} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186760546} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761105} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761108} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761112} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761113} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761121} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761123} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761143} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761144} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761145} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761152} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761154} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761157} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761169} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761170} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761172} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761174} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761192} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761201} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761203} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761204} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761217} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761218} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761219} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761224}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Two Scalar Factors in a Linear Combination"} :step/id #uuid "e9410dda-1b4e-5dca-b7ed-64fdcc297e4e" :step/math-academy-id 661408 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097488}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097488 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096653 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096452 :question/id #uuid "09d593cc-526e-5691-9a18-efff515eae59" :question/math-academy-id "e-4288" :question/problem "Consider the following diagram, where $\\overrightarrow{OA}=\\mathbf{a},$ $\\overrightarrow{OB}=\\mathbf{b},$ and $X$ divides $AB$ in the ratio $3:1.$ Find $\\overrightarrow{OX}.$\n\n![](images/67/678ea2936b8a5b1ded74cb8a0875c629e8be0f7002c98492ebf2e8109fc603ef.png)" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{OX} = \\overrightarrow{OA}+\\overrightarrow{AX}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overrightarrow{OA} = \\mathbf{a},$ so we just need to work out $\\overrightarrow{AX}.$\n\nFirst, from $\\triangle OAB,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\overset{\\to }{AO}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $AX:XB=3:1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{AX}=\\dfrac{3}{4}\\overrightarrow{AB}=\\dfrac{3}{4}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OX} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AX} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{a}+\\frac{3}{4}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\mathbf{a}+\\frac{3}{4}\\mathbf{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "feae36e7-ae88-5f3a-957f-e65a92e399ad" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761225} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097046} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097240} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256381} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256383} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256386} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256582} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256611} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256624} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294966} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294974} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302111} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302112} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302113} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302114} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302115} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302116} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302117} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302118} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302119} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329678} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387001} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390558} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420465} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514792} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514793} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514814} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514819} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514820} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514826} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514830} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514835} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514836} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514847} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186582357} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186734987} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186734990} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186734994} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186735290} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186735291} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186735294} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186735298} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186735301} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761230} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761236} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761252} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761255} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761257} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761260} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761265} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761270} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761287} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761291} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761293} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761295} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761297} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761307} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761311} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761322} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761340} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761343} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761344} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761346} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761347} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761356} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761358}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Vector That Satisfies a Ratio in Terms of Known Vectors"} :step/id #uuid "eb2bab86-218f-5b3a-9a4e-b0b3872ba6db" :step/math-academy-id 661411} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097564 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096701 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096495 :question/id #uuid "839ed5f5-165f-5de5-aba1-551ac605475f" :question/math-academy-id "e-5212" :question/problem "Consider the picture below.\n\n![](images/f2/f2518c2e998e7c7f551548a4cc7283720a66428ea8fa9872494a81cc3848ba9e.png)\n\nGiven that $\\mathbf{c} = \\lambda  \\mathbf{a} + 2 \\mathbf{b}$, find $\\lambda.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that when two known vectors are not parallel, a linear combination of them is unique. So, once we rewrite $\\mathbf c$ in terms of $\\mathbf a$ and $\\mathbf b,$ we can match coefficients to determine the unknown scalar.\n\nTo find $\\lambda,$ we want to match the expression\n$$\n\\mathbf{c} = \\lambda  \\mathbf{a} + 2 \\mathbf{b} .\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $2 \\mathbf{b} .$ So, we first draw the vector $2 \\mathbf{b}$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/ea/ea517ac4ca8e0bcf7ba48f23e66f100323d5485f1221219aacfbea9148f4a1af.png)\n\nNote that in $△ O D C$, we have:\n\n- $D C = 2 | \\mathbf{a} |$\n\n- $\\overrightarrow{D C}$ and $\\mathbf a$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overrightarrow{D C} = 2 \\mathbf{a} .$\n\nAdditionally,\n$$\n\\begin{array}{cc}\\mathbf{c} &  = \\overrightarrow{O D} + \\overrightarrow{D C} \\\\  &  = 2 \\mathbf{b} + 2 \\mathbf{a} .\\end{array}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{c} = \\lambda  \\mathbf{a} + 2 \\mathbf{b} = 2 \\mathbf{b} + 2 \\mathbf{a} .$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{a} \\nparallel \\mathbf{b},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf a,$ we get:\n$$\n\\lambda=2\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fbb8f19-f35a-59c4-9b77-8f6c3542ef28" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050467} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051014}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761359} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097164} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097181} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256370} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256371} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292806} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302041} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302311} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302312} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338753} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338754} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338756} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338757} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364880} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420466} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572666} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572695} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572704} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186735030} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186735038} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186735040} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186735386} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186735389} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186735391} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186735395} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186735397} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186735413} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186735416} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186735419} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186735423} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761363} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761366} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761370} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761374} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761384} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761389} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761400} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761402} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761409} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761411} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761426} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761427} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761435} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761436} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761442} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761447} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761452} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761459} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761462} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761463} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761464} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761469} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761474} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761475}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Scalar Factor in a Linear Combination"} :step/id #uuid "74a3f281-5081-51cb-a589-0b5531ea612f" :step/math-academy-id 661399 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362452}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362445 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362404 :tutorial/content "Now let's look at a slightly more complicated diagram.\n\nSuppose we have two vectors $\\overrightarrow{OA}=\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\overrightarrow{OB}=\\mathbf{b},$ and a parallelogram $OAXB_1,$ where $\\overrightarrow{O {B}_{1}} = 2 \\overrightarrow{O B} .$ How can we find the position vector $\\mathbf{x} = \\overrightarrow{O X}$ in terms of $\\mathbf a$ and $\\mathbf{b}?$\n\n![](images/c7/c78c8895790b28c7dbe60d0dd31a5375fef19ba2d6fa668144859e6fafaf33bb.png)\n\nAs before, we trace a path from the origin to the point $X.$ From the diagram, we can write\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} = \\overrightarrow{O {B}_{1}} + \\overrightarrow{{B}_{1} X} .\n$$\n\nWe know that\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{O {B}_{1}} = 2 \\overrightarrow{O B} = 2 \\mathbf{b} .\n$$\n\nAlso, since $OAXB_1$ is a parallelogram, opposite sides are equal and parallel. So,\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{B_1X}=\\overrightarrow{OA}=\\mathbf{a}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the expression for $\\mathbf x,$ we get\n$$\n\\begin{array}{cc}\\mathbf{x} &  = \\overrightarrow{O {B}_{1}} + \\overrightarrow{{B}_{1} X} \\\\  &  = 2 \\mathbf{b} + \\mathbf{a} \\\\  &  = \\mathbf{a} + 2 \\mathbf{b} .\\end{array}\n$$\n\nSo, even when the path uses translated copies of known vectors, we can still find the position vector by expressing each part of the path in terms of the known vectors and then adding.\n\nNow we will use the same path-tracing idea in a slightly different way. Instead of only writing a position vector, we will also use vector paths to identify unknown coefficients in linear combinations of known vectors." :tutorial/id #uuid "bd7a9db3-e422-5170-83a1-857ca34b9bc8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 15988 :tutorial/title "Writing a Position Vector Using Known Vectors"} :step/id #uuid "57d87818-8242-532e-8177-977c7fa7906e" :step/math-academy-id 661398 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097564}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362452 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362403 :tutorial/content "We can still find a position vector by tracing a path from the origin to the point and adding the vectors along that path.\n\nHowever, if one part of the path points in the opposite direction to a known vector, then we write it as a **negative** multiple of that vector.\n\nFor example, consider the picture below. \n\n![](images/d5/d5366a68ed98e7f614ff998f69098613ea8e12d05bc27274ea66e8ce5a908cb7.png)\n\nWe are given that $\\mathbf{c} = \\lambda  \\mathbf{a} + 2 \\mathbf{b}$ and we are asked to find $\\lambda.$\n\nTo find $\\lambda,$ we want to match the expression\n$$\n\\mathbf{c} = \\lambda  \\mathbf{a} + 2 \\mathbf{b} .\n$$\n\nLet $O$ be the origin, and let $\\mathbf a$ and $\\mathbf c$ end at points $A$ and $C,$ as shown in the diagram below.\n\nSince one term in the vector sum is $2 \\mathbf{b} ,$ we first draw the vector $2 \\mathbf{b}$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/bc/bc976b12b4610c69953545d95662bf5535ef2a92eb9d9cbd63e087c85f3b98e7.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $2 \\mathbf{b} .$ Then\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c} = \\overrightarrow{O P} + \\overrightarrow{P C} .\n$$\n\nFrom the construction, we have\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{O P} = 2 \\mathbf{b} .\n$$\n\nAlso, the vector $\\overrightarrow{P C}$ has length $2 | \\mathbf{a} | ,$ but points in the opposite direction to $\\mathbf a.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{P C} = - 2 \\mathbf{a} .\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the expression for $\\mathbf{c},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{cc}\\mathbf{c} &  = \\overrightarrow{O P} + \\overrightarrow{P C} \\\\  &  = 2 \\mathbf{b} + ( - 2 \\mathbf{a} ) \\\\  &  = - 2 \\mathbf{a} + 2 \\mathbf{b} .\\end{array}\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n$$\n\\mathbf{c} = \\lambda  \\mathbf{a} + 2 \\mathbf{b} = - 2 \\mathbf{a} + 2 \\mathbf{b} .\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{a} \\nparallel \\mathbf{b},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf a,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\lambda=-2.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b5155eff-3506-551b-8dc0-597bff07650f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 15990 :tutorial/title "Vectors in Opposite Directions"} :step/id #uuid "d0bece94-821b-52ea-b6b1-d2373c5447e0" :step/math-academy-id 661404 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362453}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362453 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235285 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234976 :question/id #uuid "aeaa36a2-41b5-5b7b-9797-f39df1e34050" :question/math-academy-id "e-26144" :question/problem "Consider the picture below. \n\n![](images/d4/d4f18c3567d0c55d3d6e002fbe2c6ac50b97b7adc14b016c8e95006de98ca15d.png)\n\nGiven that $\\mathbf{c} = \\lambda  \\mathbf{a} - 2 \\mathbf{b} ,$ find $\\lambda.$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda,$ we want to match the expression\n$$\n\\mathbf{c} = \\lambda  \\mathbf{a} - 2 \\mathbf{b} .\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $- 2 \\mathbf{b} .$ So, we first draw the vector $- 2 \\mathbf{b}$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/65/65a6b5743ab9b6ef820744d0d6601b7dfd8de08a3f895661d96d2fe2cab8e0a7.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $- 2 \\mathbf{b} .$ Then\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c} = \\overrightarrow{O P} + \\overrightarrow{P C} .\n$$\n\nIn $△ O P C ,$ we have:\n\n- $O P = 2 | \\mathbf{b} |$\n\n- $\\overrightarrow{OP}$ and $\\mathbf b$ have opposite directions\n\nHence,\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{O P} = - 2 \\mathbf{b} .\n$$\n\nSimilarly,\n\n- $P C = | \\mathbf{a} |$\n\n- $\\overrightarrow{P C}$ and $\\mathbf a$ have opposite directions\n\nHence,\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{P C} = - \\mathbf{a} .\n$$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{cc}\\mathbf{c} &  = \\overrightarrow{O P} + \\overrightarrow{P C} \\\\  &  = - 2 \\mathbf{b} + ( - \\mathbf{a} ) \\\\  &  = - \\mathbf{a} - 2 \\mathbf{b} .\\end{array}\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n$$\n\\mathbf{c} = \\lambda  \\mathbf{a} - 2 \\mathbf{b} = - \\mathbf{a} - 2 \\mathbf{b} .\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{a} \\nparallel \\mathbf{b},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf a,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\lambda=-1.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "58a49d00-cc58-5228-af38-9369cf31d8c8" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761476} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id